"Blocked by robots.txt\nThe following domains cannot be accessed (non-retryable error): codeberg.org--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR open data: MCP tool definitions and how they change (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/)\nciteturn25search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote MCP server in the official MCP registry once a day, and runs every new version of npm and PyPI MCP packages in a sandbox. ... File  | Rows  | Size  | What ...     JOIN 'https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/servers.jsonl.gz' s ON s.id = c.serverId ...   * A tool list is what a server showed an anonymous client on that day; servers that need a key show less or nothing.\n# MCP tool definitions and how they change\n\nHISTOR reads the tool list of every remote MCP server in the official MCP registry once a day, and runs every new version of npm and PyPI MCP packages in a sandbox. Once a week the whole record is published here as files: what each server advertised, and every change, field by field. Free under CC BY 4.0.\n\n## Latest snapshot: 2026-11-02\n\nFile  | Rows  | Size  | What\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n`servers.jsonl.gz`  | 96,923  | 21.2 MB  | One row per listed server: endpoint, transport, status, tool count, the digest of its current tool list.\n`toolsets.jsonl.gz`  | 90,641  | 1,053.9 MB  | Every tool list a server served (name, description and schemas, as served), current ones and every one a change replaced.\n`changes.jsonl.gz`  | 53,159  | 56.5 MB  | Every recorded change: tools added, removed and modified, field by field, with the signed label.\n`scans.jsonl.gz`  | 11,069  | 754 kB  | WARDEN pattern matches per tool list (rules in pattern-set.json). Leads to read, not verdicts.\n`classifications.jsonl.gz`  | 4,195  | 233 kB  | Advisory LLM readings of tool lists. Not reproducible, so never in the signed log.\n`tool_extras.jsonl.gz`  | 95,178  | 40.6 MB  | What each tool carried beyond those: annotations (readOnlyHint, destructiveHint…), _meta, title, icons. Since 2026-10-22.\n`extras_changes.jsonl.gz`  | 74,812  | 8.0 MB  | Every change to those members, annotation by annotation, with the tools that dropped readOnlyHint.\n`package_observations.jsonl.gz`  | 171,834  | 15.2 MB  | Every run of an npm/PyPI package with the version it ran and the list it served. Versions since 2026-10-26.\n`manifest.json`  |  |  | Counts and SHA-256 of every file, signed by the log's key, with the tree head of the snapshot.\n`README.md`  |  |  | The dataset card: fields, loading, citation, what the data does not say.\n\nNew here? Start with `servers.jsonl.gz` (one row per listed server) and `changes.jsonl.gz` (one row per recorded change, field by field); `toolsets.jsonl.gz` is the large one — every distinct tool list in full. Sizes are in the table.\n\nThe same files: Hugging Face (one tag per snapshot) · Zenodo (a DOI per archived snapshot; the record names its date, and latest/ here may be newer) · latest/ always points at the newest snapshot.\n\n## Load it\n    \n    from datasets import load_dataset   # Hugging Face, any snapshot tag\n    changes = load_dataset(\"alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\", data_files=\"changes.jsonl.gz\", split=\"train\")\n    \n    -- DuckDB: the servers whose tool lists changed most often\n    SELECT s.name, count(*) AS changes\n    FROM 'https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/changes.jsonl.gz' c\n    JOIN 'https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/servers.jsonl.gz' s ON s.id = c.serverId\n    GROUP BY s.name ORDER BY changes DESC LIMIT 20;\n    \n    import pandas as pd\n    servers = pd.read_json(\"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/servers.jsonl.gz\", lines=True)\n\n## Cite\n\nArtamokhov, A. (2026). HISTOR snapshot 2026-11-02: MCP tool definitions and how they change [Data set]. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23884408\n\nCite the snapshot you counted on: the same query gives other numbers on another snapshot. doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512759 stands for all of them and always opens the newest.\n\n## Check it\n\n`manifest.json` lists the SHA-256 of every file and is signed (Ed25519) by the log's key `did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9`, with the signed tree head the snapshot was taken under. Any label in it can be proven against the live log: https://histor.modelmarket.dev. Code: https://github.com/alexar76/histor.\n\n## All snapshots\n\n  * 2026-11-02 · 90,863 servers · 53,159 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23884408\n  * 2026-10-26 · 85,913 servers · 31,660 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23884119\n  * 2026-10-25 · 85,437 servers · 30,151 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23881379\n  * 2026-10-20 · 81,879 servers · 23,821 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512760\n\n## What it does not say\n\n  * A tool list is what a server showed an anonymous client on that day; servers that need a key show less or nothing.\n  * A change is a change in the definitions, not a judgement. Most are ordinary releases.\n  * Pattern matches and classifier flags are text worth reading, not proof that a server is malicious.\n  * A server operator who wants out of future snapshots can open an issue on https://github.com/alexar76/histor.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHubs — modelmarket.dev (https://modelmarket.dev/hubs)\nciteturn25search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Who modelmarket.dev knows, who is knocking, and how to run a hub of your own. ... HISTOR  | https://histor.modelmarket.dev  | —  | 3  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nLOGOS Federation Analytics  | https://logos.modelmarket.dev  | 0.2.0  | 1  | 2026-10-20 10:03  | pinned\nSignal Hunt Hub  | https://hunt.modelmarket.dev  | 3.15.16  | 6  | 2026-10-20 10:03  | pinned\nIndustrial Platform Sentinel  | https://x402-gateway-production-1f21.up.railway.app  | sentinel-federation-1.0.0  | 4  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nAttested Memory Hub  | https://hub.attestedmemory.net  | 3.15.16  | 21  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nHISTOR  | https://histor.modelmarket.dev  | —  | 3  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nIndependent AI Hub  | https://independentai.network/hub  | 3.15.16  | 33  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nmodelmarket.dev demo domain seller  | https://domains.modelmarket.dev  | —  | 1  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\nmodelmarket.dev demo eSIM seller  | https://esim.modelmarket.dev  | —  | 1  | 2026-10-20 10:04  | pinned\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/servers)\nciteturn25search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago;     curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\ ...     # -> {\"type\":\"histor.check/v1\",\"match\":\"same\"|\"different\"|\"previously-observed\"|…, ... First it reads /robots.txt (RFC 9309, product token histor, about once a day per site): a path that file does not allow HISTOR is not read, and its page here says so. Otherwise server/discover and tools/list (MCP 2026-07-28), or, for a server without that, initialize, notifications/initialized and tools/list — never a tool call, never a redirect followed on the MCP path, at most two connections per host, from a User-Agent that starts with histor/.\n### Four words used on this page\n\nTool definitions\n    The name, description and input schema of each tool a server offers — the text your model reads.\n\nPinned\n    HISTOR has recorded a fingerprint (digest) of the exact definitions it saw, so any later change is detectable.\n\nLabel\n    One signed statement about one observation: what was seen, what matched, whether it changed. Anyone can verify it offline.\n\nSigned tree head\n    The log's signed summary after each crawl. Comparing two of them proves nothing was deleted or rewritten in between.\n\nServers Changes Log Check Watch State of MCP\n\nEvery remote endpoint in the official MCP registry is on this list. “Observed” means HISTOR read its tool definitions and holds them on record; the rest are listed with the reason they could not be read — most sit behind a login, use the legacy SSE transport or need per-user values in the URL. This is the log's index, not a marketplace listing.\n\n[Input: Server name or endpoint URL]observed 41,008 changed 11,926 flagged for review 16,122 not observed 45,428 all 86,436 41,008 endpoints\n\nRemoShift Remote Jobs · com.remoshift/jobs 5 tools https://remoshift.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27FundingLandscape · io.github.tettertotter/fundinglandscape 28 tools 4 advise-tier https://fundinglandscape.com/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27Fork · app.vercel.fork-for-agents/fork 19 tools 10 advise-tier https://fork.zone/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27MovingPlace Moving Services · com.movingplace/mcp 14 tools 4 advise-tier https://mcp.movingplace.com changed 2026-10-27AgentBIT · app.agentbit/mcp 101 tools 4 advise-tier https://agentbit.app/mcp changed 2026-10-27AI Web Research & Research Brief Agent · io.github.industrial-platform-ai/research-brief 5 tools https://mcp.apify.com?tools=industrial_platform/research-brief-agent&payment=x402 changed 2026-10-27io.github.evolutionlabs-dev/signal-bureau 29 tools 11 advise-tier https://api.signalbureau.ai/api/mcp changed 2026-10-270xinsider · io.github.0xinsider/mcp 55 tools 10 advise-tier https://api.0xinsider.com/api/v1/mcp changed 2026-10-27nohumans.directory · directory.nohumans/registry 4 tools https://api.nohumans.directory/mcp changed 2026-10-27com.tkawen/intelligence-gateway 55 tools 8 advise-tier https://mcp.tkawen.com/changed 2026-10-27tgden — Telegram catalog & search · io.github.JustJuice55/telegram-catalog 6 tools https://tgden.com/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27HireAHelper Moving Services · com.hireahelper/mcp 14 tools 4 advise-tier https://mcp.hireahelper.com changed 2026-10-27Hermoso · io.github.hermoso-ai/hermoso 195 tools 1 block-tier 49 advise-tier https://app.hermoso.ai/mcp?src=registry changed 2026-10-27Multicines Ortega · com.multicinesortega/cartelera 3 tools https://multicinesortega.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27org.aspern/aspern 38 tools 1 block-tier 10 advise-tier https://aspern.org/mcp changed 2026-10-26Flaim Fantasy · app.flaim/mcp 11 tools 4 advise-tier https://api.flaim.app/mcp changed 2026-10-27Support Agent Admin by Nova (CIVAI) · co.civai.nova/support-agent-admin 93 tools https://nova.civai.co/mcp/agents/support-agent-admin changed 2026-10-26SwarmMemo · com.swarmmemo/bulletin 21 tools 5 advise-tier https://swarmmemo.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27com.jojapi/product-barcode-api 8 tools 1 advise-tier https://mcp.jojapi.com/product-barcode-api changed 2026-10-27HotelRefund Price Tracker · net.hotelrefund/price-tracker 11 tools 2 advise-tier https://hotelrefund.net/mcp changed 2026-10-27Kaiku · tech.kaiku/kaiku 185 tools 37 advise-tier https://mcp.kaiku.tech/mcp changed 2026-10-26com.thefomite/fomite 20 tools 2 advise-tier https://thefomite.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27Remote Camera · moda.ai/remote-camera 2 tools 1 advise-tier https://www.ai.moda/mcp-servers/remote-camera/mcp changed 2026-10-27SideEye Human Signal Court · site.chatgpt.bootyplease.saas-bundlecheck/sideeye 7 tools https://saas-bundlecheck.bootyplease.chatgpt.site/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27ARYSPHERE x402 chain reads and token verdicts · com.arysphere/arysphere-x402 11 tools https://api.arysphere.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27rundata.re · La Réunion 974 en direct · re.rundata/temps-reel 29 tools https://rundata.re/mcp changed 2026-10-27Homejourney Singapore property listings · sg.homejourney/listings 13 tools https://ai-apps.homejourney.sg/mcp/public changed 2026-10-27nanoodle · io.github.nanoodlecom/nanoodle-mcp 11 tools 5 advise-tier https://mcp.nanoodle.com/mcp changed 2026-10-26Speedbot Autonomous Work Network · io.github.ulasarslan6262-ui/speedbot 141 tools 1 block-tier 19 advise-tier https://speedbot.dev/mcp changed 2026-10-26Summer · ai.gosummer/summer 188 tools 22 advise-tier https://api.gosummer.ai/mcp changed 2026-10-27AINumbers Fintech Intelligence Suite · co.ainumbers/tools 754 tools 33 advise-tier https://mcp.ainumbers.co/mcp changed 2026-10-27Google Sheets Agent by Nova (CIVAI) · co.civai.nova/google-sheets-agent 9 tools https://nova.civai.co/mcp/agents/google-sheets-agent changed 2026-10-26Research Agent by Nova (CIVAI) · co.civai.nova/research-agent 4 tools https://nova.civai.co/mcp/agents/research-agent changed 2026-10-26ce-net · com.ce-net/ce-net 228 tools 13 advise-tier https://ce-net.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27io.insourcia/insourcia 21 tools https://mcp.insourcia.io/mcp changed 2026-10-27pro.particle/particle-pro 36 tools 20 advise-tier https://mcp.particle.pro changed 2026-10-27DC Hub — Data Center Energy, Electricity & Power Grid Data: Site Selection, Colocation, Gas, Fiber · cloud.dchub/mcp-server 94 tools 35 advise-tier https://dchub.cloud/mcp/registry changed 2026-10-27Google Docs Agent by Nova (CIVAI) · co.civai.nova/google-docs-agent 8 tools https://nova.civai.co/mcp/agents/google-docs-agent changed 2026-10-26A股数据 API (A-Share Data API) · com.ashareapi/ashare-data-api 85 tools 1 advise-tier https://api.ashareapi.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27Cooper Email · com.cooperemail/cooper-email 100 tools 27 advise-tier https://cooperemail.com/mcp changed 2026-10-27PreReason · com.prereason/mcp 7 tools 4 advise-tier https://api.prereason.com/api/mcp changed 2026-10-27YouSpot · com.youspot/youspot 171 tools 1 block-tier 17 advise-tier https://youspot.com/mcp/v1 changed 2026-10-24dev.mcphost/mcphost 18 tools 9 advise-tier https://mcphost.dev/mcp changed 2026-10-24Riley Craig x402 Agent Store · io.github.rccola990-cloud/x402-agent-store 89 tools 12 advise-tier https://store.agentexchange.work/mcp changed 2026-10-27Zinvyl Marketplace Agent Gateway · com.zinvyl/marketplace 14 tools 17 advise-tier https://zinvyl.com/marketplace/agent-mcp changed 2026-10-27Sirenic · eu.sirenic/sirenic 120 tools 472 advise-tier https://api.sirenic.eu/mcp changed 2026-10-27Agent Data Tools · io.github.Nutshell2000/agent-data-tools 335 tools 47 advise-tier https://agent-data-tools.revmesh2074.workers.dev/mcp changed 2026-10-27Stable Baseline · io.stablebaseline/sb 222 tools 83 advise-tier https://api.stablebaseline.io/functions/v1/cloud-serve/mcp changed 2026-10-27Taifoon coordination layer · io.taifoon/coordination-layer 15 tools 17 advise-tier https://coord.taifoon.dev/mcp?ref=mcp-registry changed 2026-10-27Taifoon coordination layer · io.taifoon/coordination-layer 15 tools 17 advise-tier https://www.taifoon.io/api/mcp?ref=mcp-registry changed 2026-10-27\n\n## Use it\n\nBefore your agent trusts a server, send the tools it received. The answer is signed by the log's did:key and names the tree head it was given under.\n    \n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …tools/list result… ],\"contribute\":true}'\n    # -> {\"type\":\"histor.check/v1\",\"match\":\"same\"|\"different\"|\"previously-observed\"|…,\n    #     \"observed\":{\"unchangedSince\":\"…\",\"changes\":0}, \"patternScan\":{…}, \"signature\":{…}}\n    \n    # README badge for your server\n    [![MCP tool defs](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/badge/<id>.svg)](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/s/<id>)\n    \n    # Atom feed of every observed change\n    https://histor.modelmarket.dev/feed.xml\n\nWARDEN checks definitions at connect\n\nHISTOR remembers what was advertised\n\nTHEMIS admits at publish\n\nHub lists histor.check@v1\n\nAWR the signed format\n\n## Questions\n\nIs this a marketplace listing?\n\nNo. The server list is the log's index: every remote endpoint in the official MCP registry, with what HISTOR could observe about it. Nothing here is for sale and nothing is ranked by quality.\n\nDoes HISTOR tell me a server is safe?\n\nNo, and it never will. It tells you what a server advertised, whether that changed, and which published patterns matched the text. Whether to trust the server is your decision — HISTOR makes sure you decide on the same facts as everyone else.\n\nWhy are so many servers \"not observed\"?\n\nMost remote MCP endpoints require a login (they answer 401 to anyone without a key), some use the legacy SSE transport or need per-user values in the URL, and some are simply down. They stay on the list with the reason, so the numbers never hide them.\n\nHow do I check a server I already use?\n\nOpen the Check tab, paste the server's endpoint and the tools/list result your client received, and press Check. You get back, signed, whether HISTOR observed the same definitions — or different ones, or an older set.\n\nHow do I get a badge for my server?\n\nFind your endpoint on the Servers tab, open it, and copy the Markdown snippet under the facts. The badge shows the date your definitions were pinned or last changed, and links to the full history.\n\nWhy should I trust HISTOR itself?\n\nYou do not have to. Every label and every tree head is signed with one published key; the Log tab proves in your browser that the log only grew; and the command-line auditor does the same from any machine. What remains is that one operator runs it today — a second, independent observer is the next step.\n\nWhat does it cost?\n\nLooking things up, the badges, the feed and /check are free. HISTOR also offers histor.check@v1 to the AIMarket Hub, so agents can reach it through the market.\n\nI run an MCP server. What does HISTOR send it, and how do I opt out?\n\nEvery 6 hours, and every 30 minutes for a week once its tools have changed twice in a week. First it reads /robots.txt (RFC 9309, product token histor, about once a day per site): a path that file does not allow HISTOR is not read, and its page here says so. Otherwise server/discover and tools/list (MCP 2026-07-28), or, for a server without that, initialize, notifications/initialized and tools/list — never a tool call, never a redirect followed on the MCP path, at most two connections per host, from a User-Agent that starts with histor/. To keep HISTOR out, disallow your MCP path for User-agent: histor (or for all crawlers); to let HISTOR alone in, add User-agent: histor with an Allow line for that path. An issue on the histor repository with your endpoint or host also works: it is then listed as not observed (operator-opt-out), with the reason, rather than silently dropped. Labels already in the log stay: the log only grows.\n\nHISTOR\n\nἵστωρ — the one who knows because they saw. A public, append-only record of what MCP servers advertised, and when it changed.\n\n##### Docs\n\nREADME What a label says Auditing the log API\n\n##### Network\n\nHub · modelmarket.dev WARDEN Alien Monitor GitHub\n\nHISTOR · MIT · part of the AIMarket ecosystem did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWE…--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/log)\nciteturn25search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Every day HISTOR reads what each remote (streamable-HTTP) endpoint in the official MCP registry advertises, fingerprints it, signs what it saw and appends it to a log that can only grow. ... People who use MCP servers in Claude, Cursor or any client — check a server before you connect it, and again when it changes. ...     The name, description and input schema of each tool a server offers — the text your model reads.\n\nTransparency log · MCP tool definitions\n\n# Every MCP server's tools, on the record.\n\nYour AI client trusts the tool descriptions an MCP server sends it — and a server can change them after you approved it. HISTOR keeps a public, signed record of what every remote MCP server in the official registry advertised, and tells you the day it changed.\n\nRemote endpoints: it lists tools and never calls one. Registry packages: bounded canary calls run inside the isolated sandbox. It never says a server is safe — it tells you what was said, and when.\n\nWhat HISTOR is for\n\n## Tool descriptions are instructions. They should not change behind your back.\n\nThe problem\n\n### A server can change what your model reads\n\nWhen you connect an MCP server, its tool descriptions go straight to the model as instructions. A server can pass your review with harmless descriptions and change them next week — one added sentence is all a prompt injection needs, and most clients never ask you again.\n\nWhat HISTOR does\n\n### It keeps the public record\n\nEvery day HISTOR reads what each remote (streamable-HTTP) endpoint in the official MCP registry advertises, fingerprints it, signs what it saw and appends it to a log that can only grow. When a server's definitions change, the change gets a date and a diff.\n\nWhat you get\n\n### A second pair of eyes you can check\n\nLook up any server before you trust it. Paste what your client received and learn whether everyone else sees the same thing. Follow changes in a feed, or put a badge in your README. Every answer is signed, so you can verify it instead of believing it.\n\n### Who it is for\n\nPeople who use MCP servers in Claude, Cursor or any client — check a server before you connect it, and again when it changes.\n\nAgent builders whose agents connect to servers on their own — call /check before trusting a tool set.\n\nRegistries and directories that want more than stars and download counts — show when a listing's tools last changed.\n\nServer authors who want to show a stable, documented history — add the badge, and every change you ship is dated in public.\n\n### Four words used on this page\n\nTool definitions\n    The name, description and input schema of each tool a server offers — the text your model reads.\n\nPinned\n    HISTOR has recorded a fingerprint (digest) of the exact definitions it saw, so any later change is detectable.\n\nLabel\n    One signed statement about one observation: what was seen, what matched, whether it changed. Anyone can verify it offline.\n\nSigned tree head\n    The log's signed summary after each crawl. Comparing two of them proves nothing was deleted or rewritten in between.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFour filings, zero merges: where three agents actually sit this week (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/four-filings-zero-merges-where-three-agents-actually-sit-this-week/)\nciteturn25search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; It scans advertised tool definitions — injection, exfiltration, secret requests — and records an allow or block before those definitions reach the model. ... The desk is histor.modelmarket.dev.\n\n3 Oct 2026 · 3 min read\n\n# Four filings, zero merges: where three agents actually sit this week\n\nBetween 1 and 3 October 2026 I filed the same three names into four public catalogs. None of those pull requests is merged. The software is already callable. The directories are not.\n\nThe three names are WARDEN (an MCP firewall for tool definitions), HISTOR (a signed log of what remote MCP servers advertised), and AIMarket Hub (the catalog they sit in). This is a status note, not a launch.\n\n## What was filed\n\nWhere  | When  | What  | State on 3 Oct 2026\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nDocker MCP Registry #5364  | 1 Oct  | WARDEN, local server, package 0.8.2  | Open\nDocker MCP Registry #5365  | 1 Oct  | Hub at `https://modelmarket.dev/mcp`  | Open\nawesome-erc8004 #120  | 1 Oct  | Hub, HISTOR, WARDEN as ERC-8004 agents on Base  | Open\ncoinbase/x402 #373  | 2 Oct  | Six paid routes under Services/Endpoints  | Open\nCline marketplace #2735  | 3 Oct  | WARDEN server submission  | Open\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\naimarket-histor · PyPI (https://pypi.org/project/aimarket-histor/)\nciteturn25search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Live: histor.modelmarket.dev · Landing: alexar76.github.io/histor · Capabilities: `histor.check@v1` · `histor.server@v1` · `histor.changes@v1` · Port: `9490` ... Each snapshot's README lists the fields and loads in pandas or DuckDB (histor/dataset.py).\n\n# HISTOR\n\nImage: Python >=3.11 Image: Postgres in production, SQLite for development Image: MTL/1 labels on AWR/2 Image: RFC 9162 Merkle log Image: Documentation in 5 languages\n\nHISTOR (ἵστωρ, “the one who knows because they saw”) — a public transparency log of MCP tool definitions\nWhat every remote MCP endpoint in the official registry advertised · when it changed · signed, appended, auditable\n\nEnglish · Русский · Español · Français · 中文\n\n> WARDEN quality cycle: MOMUS → AI-Factory → SKOPOS → shared node agent → deployment.\n\nLive: histor.modelmarket.dev · Landing: alexar76.github.io/histor · Capabilities: `histor.check@v1` · `histor.server@v1` · `histor.changes@v1` · Port: `9490`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions | VibeLeaderboard (https://www.vibeleaderboard.ai/app/d8a7cafb-d59e-470a-9207-e89df4720091)\nciteturn25search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; histor.modelmarket.dev ... The site says HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry each day and runs each new npm and PyPI MCP package version in a sandbox. ...     A manifest of file checksums, signed with Ed25519 ...     Servers that require a key show less or nothing to the anonymous client\n\n← Back to Tools\n\nIndex / tool\n\n# HISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions\n\nhistor.modelmarket.dev\n\nVisit histor.modelmarket.dev\n\nCategory\n    Cybersecurity\n\nRank\n    \n\nNo. 2267Tools index\n\nPrevious survey · No. 2094 · 30 October 2026\n\nPricing\n    Open Source\n\nType\n    TOOL\n\nUse case\n    Security & Identity\n\nDate\n    Published Oct 22, 2026\n\n## About\n\nMonthly snapshots of a public transparency log covering remote MCP servers in the official registry and npm and PyPI MCP packages. The files hold the tool definitions each server served, every recorded change field by field, pattern scan matches, and advisory classifier readings. A signed manifest lists checksums so users can verify files, and the data is released under CC BY 4.0.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe site says HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry each day and runs each new npm and PyPI MCP package version in a sandbox. It presents its pattern matches and classifier flags as leads worth reading, not as proof that a server is malicious.\n\n### Stated on the product site\n\nLicense\n    CC BY 4.0\n\nRelease cadence\n    A full snapshot published as files once a month\n\nVerification\n    A manifest of file checksums, signed with Ed25519\n\nMirrors\n    Hugging Face with one tag per snapshot, Zenodo with a DOI per snapshot\n\nCoverage limit\n    Servers that require a key show less or nothing to the anonymous client\n\nCode link\n    The page links a GitHub repository for the project\n\n### Not stated on the site\n\n  * The page does not name the LLM behind the advisory classifier readings.\n  * The page does not say what the sandbox run of a package records beyond its tool list.\n  * How server operators' opt-out requests are handled beyond opening an issue is not described on the page.\n\nWritten from the product site at histor.modelmarket.dev.\n\n## Tags\n\nmcp dataset transparency-log tool-definitions supply-chain security--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n45% of remote MCP servers changed their tool definitions in four weeks. The log is now a dataset. (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/45-of-remote-mcp-servers-changed-their-tool-definitions-in-four-weeks-the-log-is-now-a-dataset/)\nciteturn25search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Servers that need a key show their public tool list or none. ... Fields that vary in shape are JSON text, so every file loads as a flat table. ... To check one server without downloading anything: modelmarket.dev/tools/histor-check.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions · Datasets at Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/datasets/alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions)\nciteturn25search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Live view and per-server pages: https://histor.modelmarket.dev · code: https://github.com/alexar76/histor ... `manifest.json` holds the full hashes and the counts, signed by the log's key `did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9` (type `histor.dataset/v1`, Ed25519 over RFC 8785 JSON). ... Fields whose shape varies between servers are JSON text (`*Json`), so every file loads as a flat table. ...   * servers — `id` (stable per name+endpoint), `name`, `title`, `description`, `endpoint` (`https://…` or `npm:`/`pypi:` for packages), `transport`, `registry`, `version`, `repository`, `firstListed`/`lastListed`, `delisted`, `skipReason` (why it was not dialled), `lastStatus` and `lastDetail` (the last attempt's outcome), `lastObserved`, `toolCount`, `toolSetDigest` (join to toolsets), `firstPinned`, `unchangedSince`, `attempts`, `observations`, `changes`, `blockMatches`/`adviseMatches`/`recordMatches`, `classifierFlags`, `chainLabel` (latest label id), `page`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR open data review — is it legit & production-ready? | Legit.Show (https://legit.show/s/histor-modelmarket-dev)\nciteturn25search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Monthly open dataset tracking MCP server tool definitions and their changes over time. ... Legit.Show ran its deterministic 7-Frame production-readiness benchmark on HISTOR open data (public-surface assessment), measured from the public surface with no LLM in the scoring path.\n-------------------------------------------------------------------